WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.640
How does repeated squaring avoid

2
00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:04.860
enormous loops? It halves a

3
00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:06.920
binary exponent at each step,

4
00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:09.160
reusing squared powers instead of

5
00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:11.420
multiplying once per exponent value.

6
00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:14.440
We will trace modular exponentiation

7
00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:16.340
and transfer the same idea

8
00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:18.560
to permutations and Fibonacci numbers

9
00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:20.380
modulo a given modulus.

10
00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:24.020
For modular exponentiation, maintain a

11
00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:26.100
result r, a current base

12
00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:27.860
b and a remaining exponent

13
00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:30.160
e. Initially r is one,

14
00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:32.120
b is the reduced input

15
00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:33.900
base, and e is the

16
00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:36.520
desired exponent. The invariant is

17
00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:38.060
that r times b to

18
00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:39.900
e has the same residue

19
00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.000
as the original power.

20
00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.400
At an odd exponent, multiply

21
00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:45.680
the result by the current

22
00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.200
base modulo p. Then square

23
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:49.720
the base modulo p and

24
00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:51.260
replace the exponent by its

25
00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:53.240
integer half. At an even

26
00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:55.920
exponent, skip the result multiplication

27
00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:57.940
but perform the same squaring

28
00:00:57.940 --> 00:00:58.820
and halving.

29
00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:01.100
Trace two to the thirteen

30
00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:03.300
modulo five. Starting with result

31
00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:05.420
one, base two and exponent

32
00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:07.760
thirteen, the odd step changes

33
00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:09.560
the result to two, squares

34
00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:10.880
the base to four and

35
00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:12.340
halves the exponent to six.

36
00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:14.520
The next even step keeps

37
00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:16.940
result two, squares four to

38
00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:18.800
one modulo five and halves

39
00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:19.780
six to three.

40
00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:22.360
The remaining odd rounds multiply

41
00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:23.920
the result by one, so

42
00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:25.500
it stays two while the

43
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:27.200
exponent becomes one and then

44
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:29.600
zero. At exponent zero, the

45
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:31.320
invariant leaves only the result,

46
00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:33.020
proving the answer is two.

47
00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:36.200
Four bit-processing rounds replaced thirteen

48
00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:38.400
repeated multiplications in this small

49
00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:38.860
example.

50
00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:41.260
There are logarithmically many rounds

51
00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:43.220
because e is halved, but

52
00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:44.580
that is only the operation

53
00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:47.140
count. Reducing after every multiplication

54
00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.960
keeps residues to order log

55
00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:50.920
p bits, so standard integer

56
00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:53.140
arithmetic makes each round polynomial

57
00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:54.440
in the input bit lengths.

58
00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:56.740
Computing an unreduced giant power

59
00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:58.360
first would lose that essential

60
00:01:58.360 --> 00:01:59.440
operand-size bound.

61
00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:03.600
Permutation powering uses composition instead

62
00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:05.820
of multiplication, with the identity

63
00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:09.260
permutation as exponent-zero result. A

64
00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:11.280
permutation on k elements stays

65
00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.160
a k-element list after every

66
00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:15.180
composition, so entries do not

67
00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:17.840
grow into enormous integers. Repeated

68
00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:20.380
squaring gives logarithmically many compositions,

69
00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:22.280
each polynomial in that list’s

70
00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:22.880
encoding.

71
00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:25.900
For Fibonacci numbers, use the

72
00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:27.600
two-by-two matrix with rows one

73
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:30.060
one and one zero. Its

74
00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:32.000
nth power has Fibonacci n

75
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:34.080
in the upper-right entry, including

76
00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.140
exponent zero where that entry

77
00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:38.580
is zero. Reduce entries modulo

78
00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:40.780
p during every matrix multiplication,

79
00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:43.100
keeping the whole computation polynomial

80
00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:44.560
in log n and log

81
00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:44.920
p.

82
00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:47.440
The photos also show exact

83
00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:49.880
integer matrix powering, where bare

84
00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:51.680
multiplication count is not a

85
00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:54.600
complete complexity proof. Matrix entries

86
00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:56.040
can become enormous if they

87
00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:57.700
are not reduced, and writing

88
00:02:57.700 --> 00:02:59.900
their bits costs time. The

89
00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:02.560
modular Fibonacci and bounded-entry permutation

90
00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:04.640
examples make the missing size

91
00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:05.720
condition explicit.

92
00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:07.700
Things to take note of.

93
00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:10.680
State the invariant, handle exponent

94
00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:12.500
zero, halve the exponent and

95
00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:14.760
reuse computed half-powers rather than

96
00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:17.140
recomputing both recursively. In a

97
00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:20.020
P-membership proof, bound encoded operand

98
00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:21.100
lengths as well as the

99
00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:23.240
number of arithmetic operations; that

100
00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:24.920
is what makes repeated squaring

101
00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:26.000
an efficiency argument.
